From 008809cbdbe1f2f3a4e2b66a94f6d88ab1fb4f0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:15:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Hades: your display at the first start and in Check this
 setup, and share my results

Hades sets the picture for a display from what its EDID declares, and asks nothing. Two things no EDID says: whether
the display is a projector, and how bright it is; and whether the user wants HDR videos as HDR at all. Users who
needed them did not find the settings (forum thread 30682, #175 and #195: a projector that took SDR, "Play HDR videos
as SDR" far from "Adjust display HDR mode"), and one with a TV remote that did not work did not know that the CEC
adapter was on another output than the picture (issue 24).

"Your display" (modules/display.py, a page of the first-start wizard after the services; and a button of System >
Check this setup, for later) says what Hades found: the display and its connector, what it takes (Dolby Vision and
its version, HDR10, HLG), whether a sound bar or receiver is between (by the HDMI address of the EDID), and where the
TV's remote reaches the PC over HDMI-CEC: an adapter of the kernel on the output of the picture, on another output
(then why the remote does not reach Hades, and what to do), a USB-CEC adapter of Pulse-Eight, or none. The name of
an adapter is read with CEC_ADAP_G_CAPS, without becoming its follower or initiator: nothing is sent, libCEC is not
disturbed. Then two buttons:
- Display: TV, Projector (and its peak: 50 to 300 nits, or "I don't know", 100) or Monitor. A projector that takes
  HDR10 and not Dolby Vision gets what docs/settings.md says for it: Dolby Vision adapted to it at its peak, HDR10
  videos brighter than it adapted (Automatic), and the note to turn off its own dynamic tone mapping; one that takes
  Dolby Vision maps it itself and nothing of Dolby Vision changes; every projector gets the SDR made for it (white at
  100 nits) where HDR goes out as SDR.
- HDR videos (only where the display takes HDR): as HDR (the default), HLG as SDR, or all as SDR with Dolby Vision
  too ("Adjust display HDR mode" and "Native Dolby Vision output" off).
Going on without pressing them changes nothing. What a choice changes is written to a journal first (its value
before), so that another answer puts it back: TV and "as HDR" leave every setting as it was before the step. Where
the journal cannot be written nothing is changed. kodi.log says what was found and what was set.

"Share my results" (System > Check this setup) shows one line of LibreELEC Hades and its version, the CPU, the GPU and
its driver (GL_RENDERER and GL_VERSION of kodi.log, without the versions of LLVM, DRM and the kernel), each display
(its EDID name, which has no serial number, its connector, what it takes, a sound bar or receiver between) and the
results of the last check in a few words, and copies it to a USB drive as hades-results.txt, to paste in the forum or
in an issue. Names of hardware and results only: no serial number, no address or name of a network, no name of the PC
or of a user, no path; a last guard takes out anything shaped like an IP or MAC address. Collecting "What has been
tested" from users was done by hand from their logs. The same line is kept beside the results of each check
(share.txt), which a diagnostic report takes too.
